#{[17592186095258 :answer/value "-\\frac{{3}^{x+6}}{\\operatorname{ln}3}+C"] [17592186095259 :answer/value "-\\frac{{3}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}3}+C"] [17592186095260 :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{-x-6}}{\\operatorname{ln}3}+C"] [17592186095388 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095392] [17592186095389 :answer/value "2x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x"] [17592186095390 :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}x(2x+\\operatorname{sec}x)"] [17592186095391 :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x"] [17592186095393 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}x(2+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x)"] [17592186095394 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095399] [17592186095396 :answer/value "-3{x}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}2}"] [17592186095398 :answer/value "-3{x}^{-4}+\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}2}"] [17592186095406 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095408] [17592186095424 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095425] [17592186095472 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095474] [17592186095484 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095489] [17592186095664 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095669] [17592186096972 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x^5-4x^2$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=3u^2u^{\\prime}\\\\&=3(3x^5-4x^2)^2(15x^4-8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186097020 :question/worked-solution "On an interval avoiding $x=1$, rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=-\\frac2{(x-1)^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac2{x-1}+C.\n$$\n\nThe condition $y(2)=1$ gives $1=2+C$, so $C=-1$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=\\frac2{x-1}-1,\\qquad x>1.\n$$\n\nThe interval $(1,\\infty)$ is the maximal interval containing the initial point."] [17592186097099 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 3 e^{- 6 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-6x} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 6 x}}{2}+C\\right)=3 e^{- 6 x}.\n$$"] [17592186097118 :question/worked-solution "Rearrange as $f^{\\prime}(x)=-11x$ and integrate:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\int-11x\\,\\mathrm dx=-\\frac{11}{2}x^2+C.\n$$"] [17592186097154 :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186097162 :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}^{2}}{\\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}\\right)=\\frac{10 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x \\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186097220 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 2^{6 x - 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{2^{6x - 1}}{6\\ln 2} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2^{6 x - 1}}{6 \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}+C\\right)=2^{6 x - 1}.\n$$"] [17592186097221 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 3^{- x - 6}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\frac{3^{-x - 6}}{\\ln 3} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{3^{- x - 6}}{\\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}+C\\right)=3^{- x - 6}.\n$$"] [17592186097244 :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2} \\tan{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=x^{2} \\left(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right) + 2 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186097245 :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{3} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\log{\\left(x \\right)}}{x^{4} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}} + \\frac{1}{x^{4} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186097247 :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x}\\right)=\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given point gives\n\n$$\n4.\n$$"] [17592186097250 :question/worked-solution "With $u=x^4+3x$, we have $f=u^2$ and $u^{\\prime}=4x^3+3$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=2(x^4+3x)(4x^3+3).\n$$"] [17592186097258 :question/worked-solution "Write $y=(4x-3)^{1/2}$ and apply the chain rule:\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}=\\frac12(4x-3)^{-1/2}\\cdot4=\\frac2{\\sqrt{4x-3}}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative is defined for $x>3/4$."] [17592186097260 :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule, $(uv)′=u′v+uv′$, and differentiate each factor:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(8 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)=8 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 24 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given point gives\n\n$$\n6 \\pi^{2}.\n$$"]}
